[SC1b WHAT SURVIVES — the true lemma, with the two hypotheses the first draft never stated]
  LEMMA.  Let C be a FINITE TRANSITIVE strict order.  Then every
  SKY-A and SKY-C row is NON-EMPTY, because d47a writes the trace
  of f as {c in dirs : c == f or C[c][f]} — the REFLEXIVE clause
  `c == f` is load-bearing (a cover lies above no cover, so the
  first draft's stated reason is false for the covers themselves),
  and transitivity is what carries a non-cover f down to a cover.
  CONSEQUENCE, and it is ALL the empty-row argument buys: a SKY-A
  or SKY-C sky can never shatter its FULL direction set (k =
  |dirs|).  It says NOTHING about proper subsets.
  the 21-event poset: transitive = True, SKY-A |dirs| = 5, |rows| = 16, EMPTY ROW PRESENT = False
  hypothesis 1 (reflexivity): the SAME poset with the `c == f` clause removed has an empty row = True
  hypothesis 2 (transitivity): on 0<1,0<2,0<3,1<2,2<3 (NOT 1<3), d47a SKY-A dirs = [1], rows = [[], [1]], EMPTY ROW = True
  [PASS] SC1b THE SURVIVING LEMMA, with both hypotheses exhibited.  On a finite TRANSITIVE order every SKY-A/SKY-C row is non-empty — the reflexive `c == f` clause and transitivity are each load-bearing, and dropping either one produces an empty row (both counter-examples printed above).  Hence a SKY-A/SKY-C sky can never shatter its FULL direction set.  That is the WHOLE content of the empty-row argument; SC3 shows it does not extend to proper subsets  (21-event poset transitive = True, empty row = False; empty row without reflexivity = True; empty row without transitivity = True; full-direction-set shatterings over 519 SKY-A/SKY-C skies on D47's own records = 0)

